Up to now, there are a number of commercial cell surface fluorescent stains, which can be simply classified into two types by how these reagents interact with the cell surfaces: (1) hydrophobic/nonspecific interaction-based stains and (2) specific recognition-based stains (Wang et al., 2016a). The hydrophobic/nonspecific interaction-based stains such as 1,1'-dioctadecyl-3,3,3',3'-tetramethyl-indodicarbocyanine perchlorate (DiD), N-(3-triethylammoniumpropyl)-4-(6-(4-(diethylamino)phenyl)hexatrienyl)pyridiniumdi bromide (FM4-64), and CellMask families have been frequently used for membrane staining, especially the plasma membranes of mammalian cells. However, there are some drawbacks for these fluorescent probes. For instance, their poor photobleaching resistance may limit the use in long-term tracking,
and most of these probes are prone to rapid cellular internalization with short retention time in the plasma membranes (Jia et al., 2016; Wang et al., 2015, 2016b). As for the specific recognition-based stains, they label the cell contours by selectively binding to some plasma membrane structures/components. For instance, wheat germ agglutinin (WGA, a lectin) can recognize specific sugars (N-acetyl-D-glucosamine and N-acetyl-D-neuraminic acid residues) on cell surfaces (Pellegrina et al., 2005). Hence, the fluorescence-labeled WGA is commonly used to stain mammalian plasma membranes or bacterial cell walls (Emde et al., 2014; Ernst et al., 2013; Sayed et al., 2020). Nevertheless, since cell surface sugars vary in different mammalian cells, the fluorescent WGA conjugates cannot effectively stain all cell types. To overcome the above drawbacks, universal, stable, and multicolor cell surface fluorescence imaging probes are highly demanded.
