hu et al., 2016). Regarding the visualization of epidermal cells in zebrafish, there are two common strategies which include (1) constructing transgenic zebrafish expressing fluorescent proteins on plasma membranes and (2) fixing zebrafish for immunofluorescence staining (Blaser et al., 2006; Lepage et al., 2014). However, the first approach is time-consuming and complex, and the latter one fails to realize real-time imaging of living zebrafish. Therefore, developing more convenient and high-efficiency in situ plasma membrane imaging strategies is imperative. Notably, our previous work has offered a facile strategy to satisfy the above need (Jia et al., 2019b), and four probes (Chol-PEG-dye, dye = fluorescein, nitrobenzoxadiazole, rhodamine, and cyanine5 (Cy5)) with different fluorescence colors were applied for cell imaging. However, only Chol-PEG-Cy5 presents desirable wash-free cell membrane imaging performance due to the formation of dimers, which limits the further bioimaging applications of Chol-PEG-dye when the samples such as cells need to be costained by other red fluorescent dyes, and Chol-PEG-Cy5 can only be retained on the cell surface for a short time period (2 h). Thus, the development of multicolor fluorescent probes and long-term in situ plasma membrane imaging techniques are still required which will significantly impact the future studies on zebrafish.
Dendrimer-based nanoparticles (NPs) that are suitable for fluorescence imaging applications have been widely reported (Chen et al., 2020; Li et al., 2019; Saravanan et al., 2011; Srikun et al., 2011; Talanov et al., 2006; Xu et al., 2007). The dendrimeric NPs can be fabricated with different sizes and functions by conjugating the terminal groups of dendrimers with other molecules (Hu et al., 2012; Ren et al., 2020). Owing to their well-defined structure and molecular weight, dendrimers are suitable candidates to be prepared into fluorescent nanoprobes. To our knowledge, however, dendrimer-based nanoprobes intended for cell membrane fluorescence imaging are still very rare, because dendrimers are easily internalized by cells due to their small sizes (usually smaller than \(10\ \mathrm{nm}\)) and positive surface charges (Fox et al., 2018; Xu et al., 2019). In this work, we designed a series of wash-free and multicolor fluorescent probes based on dendrimers for universal and long-term plasma membrane imaging. The prepared probes were fabricated via two steps (Scheme 1). Firstly, cholesterol-poly(ethylene glycol) \(_{2k}\)-N-hydroxysuccinimide ester (Chol-PEG-NHS) was conjugated with
amine-terminated generation 4 (G4-NH2) poly(amidoamine) (PAMAM) dendrimer to obtain dendrimer-PEG-Chol (termed DPC NPs). Then, different fluorescent dyes (i.e., cyanine2 (Cy2, in the form of Cy2-COOH), cyanine3 (Cy3, in the form of NHS-Cy3), and Cy5 (in the form of NHS-Cy5)) were separately conjugated onto DPC NPs to afford DPC-Cy2 (green-fluorescent), DPC-Cy3 (yellow-fluorescent), and DPC-Cy5 (red-fluorescent) NPs. In our design, these probes consist of a dendrimer core, several hydrophilic PEG chains for ensuring water dispersity and biocompatibility, many hydrophobic cholesterol anchors (as the terminal moieties of the PEG segments) for membrane insertion, and fluorescent dyes for membrane visualization. Two factors ensure the excellent plasma membrane staining performance of the probes we develop: (1) The obtained probes possess suitable sizes and decreased positive charges compared with neat dendrimers, which enables the probes to stain the plasma membranes by electrostatic interaction. (2) The abundant cholesterol moieties of the probes which can insert into the lipid bilayer of the plasma membrane by hydrophobic interaction make the probes achieve long membrane retention time. We will show in the following section that these probes can satisfactorily stain the plasma membranes of a variety of mammalian cells in a wash-free manner and for a long time, and can also realize the successful visualization of the epidermal cell membranes in zebrafish. Additionally, we also demonstrate that such a dendrimer-based membrane imaging technique is applicable to the in vivo toxicity evaluation of nanomaterials, as exemplified by metal–organic frameworks (MOFs).
## 2. Results and discussion
## 2.1. Characterization and cytotoxicity evaluation
Afterwards, the fluorescence emission spectra of DPC-Cy2, DPC-Cy3, and DPC-Cy5 suspensions were measured (Fig. S1c). Moreover, the hydrodynamic sizes of DPC-Cy2, DPC-Cy3, and DPC-Cy5 dispersed in phosphate-buffered saline (PBS) (10 mM, pH = 7.4) were measured within one week (Fig. S1d), and the results confirmed the good colloidal stability of these probes. Further, the transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images in Fig. 1a and Fig. S2a and b revealed that the three NPs all possessed a spherical nanostructure, and the hydrodynamic sizes, as measured by dynamic light scattering (DLS), of DPC-Cy2, DPC-Cy3, and DPC-Cy5 were \(24.4 \pm 4.5\), \(28.2 \pm 3.7\), and \(27.5 \pm 4.3\ \mathrm{nm}\), respectively (Fig. S2c). Due to the presence of unreacted amino groups of dendrimers, the above probes were slightly positively charged, as demonstrated by the zeta potential results (Fig. 1b). To check whether the fluorescent dyes were successfully conjugated to DPC NPs, the ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) spectra of the three NP suspensions after reaction were collected, and the corresponding curves (Fig. 1c) revealed different characteristic absorption peaks of the Cy2, Cy3, and Cy5 dyes, suggesting the successful synthesis of the DPC-Cy2, DPC-Cy3, and DPC-Cy5 NPs. To be suitable for use as cell imaging probes, the dendrimer dyes should have slight or no cytotoxicity during the staining procedure. Herein, the three probes (DPC-Cy2, DPC-Cy3, and DPC-Cy5 NPs) at different concentrations were incubated with HPAEpiCs (human pulmonary alveolar epithelial cells) for 1 h, and the 3-(4,5-dimethyl-2-thiazolyl)-2,5-diphenyl-2H-tetrazolium bromide (MTT) assay results
revealed no cytotoxicity of these probes (Fig. 1d). Additionally, MTT assay of a longer incubation time period \((24\,\mathrm{h})\) was also carried out, and the results also revealed negligible cytotoxicity of these probes (Fig. S2d).
## 2.2. Multicolor fluorescence imaging of mammalian cells
In view of the similar molecular weight and structure of DPC-Cy2, DPC-Cy3, and DPC-Cy5 NPs, the DPC-Cy5 probe was taken as an example for investigating the staining conditions and mechanism. According to the results in Fig. S3a, HPAEpiCs could be well stained by DPC-Cy5 suspension at a concentration of \(30\,\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\). Surprisingly, it was also found that skipping the PBS washing step during the staining process did not result in severe background interference to the imaging results even at a high concentration of \(120\,\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\), indicating the wash-free performance of the DPC-Cy5 probe. To study the appropriate incubation time, HPAEpiCs were treated with DPC-Cy5 suspension \((30\,\upmu\mathrm{g/mL})\) for different time periods, and the confocal imaging results revealed that DPC-Cy5 could stain HPAEpiCs in 5 min which suggested the highly efficient staining capacity of DPC-Cy5 (Fig. S3b). If the cells were washed by PBS, the probes could still maintain high cell membrane imaging quality for at least \(8\,\mathrm{h}\) (Fig. S4). According to the previous reports, cholesterol plays a key role in cell surface anchoring due to its high binding affinity for plasma membrane (Jia et al., 2017, 2018). To demonstrate the important role of cholesterol in the plasma membrane staining in the present work, Cy5-conjugated dendrimers (dendrimer-Cy5) were prepared for comparison purpose. The morphologies
of dendrimer and dendrimer-Cy5 were observed by TEM, and their sizes/zeta potential values were measured by a zetasizer. The results indicated that dendrimer and dendrimer-Cy5 had similar size/morphology and surface charge (Fig. S5a–c). Then, free Cy5 molecules (in the form of NHS-Cy5, which might be converted to Cy5-COOH in an aqueous solution), Cy5-conjugated dendrimers (dendrimer-Cy5), and DPC-Cy5 NPs were incubated with HPAEpiCs, respectively. As shown in Fig. 2a, free Cy5 molecules could not stain the plasma membranes, and dendrimer-Cy5 achieved much worse plasma membrane imaging quality than DPC-Cy5, confirming the significant role of cholesterol in plasma membrane imaging. On the other hand, to prove that the electrostatic attraction (between the positively charged DPC-Cy5 probe and the negatively charged cell surface) was also crucial for the staining performance, we consumed the residual amine groups of DPC-Cy5 by reacting with formaldehyde to obtain spherical and negatively charged FDPC-Cy5 (Fig. S5a–c), which exhibited weaker cellular fluorescence intensity than DPC-Cy5 (Fig. S5d and e), thus demonstrating our above conjecture. To further verify the imaging quality of DPC-Cy5, the commercial cell membrane dyes of CellMask Green and DiD were chosen as the control groups. It was observed that DPC-Cy5 presented better plasma membrane imaging quality and longer retention time than the two commercial cell membrane dyes (Fig. 2b). In detail, CellMask Green and DiD were massively endocytosed by the cells after \(1\,\mathrm{h}\), but DPC-Cy5 NPs were still retained on the plasma membrane. In addition, the confocal images were further analyzed by the ImageJ software, and the results suggested that the DPC-Cy5-stained group presented a higher signal/background ratio (Fig. 2c–e), which confirmed the satisfactory imaging outcome of the probe. For fluorescent probes, photostability is an important characteristic that is crucial for realizing stable imaging performance. Herein, we compared the imaging results of the DPC-Cy5-stained cells with those of the DiD-stained cells under continuous laser irradiation \((638\,\mathrm{nm}, 30\%\) of the laser output), and the confocal imaging results (Fig. S6) revealed that DPC-Cy5 possessed higher photostability than the commercial dye (DiD).
Inspired by the above results, the long-term imaging performance of DPC-Cy5 was further explored. As displayed in Fig. 3a, DPC-Cy5
achieved long-term plasma membrane imaging and could be stably retained on the cell surfaces for at least \(4\,\mathrm{~h~}\) before being partially internalized, and the plasma membranes could still be clearly recognized even after \(8\,\mathrm{~h~}\). In fact, the cholesterol molecules of DPC-Cy5 can tightly insert into the lipid bilayers so that the DPC-Cy5 probes will be retained on the plasma membrane for a long time.
In addition, the DPC-Cy2, DPC-Cy3, and DPC-Cy5 probes achieved universal plasma membrane labeling for different types of mammalian cells including DC2.4 (a murine dendritic cell line), B16 (a murine melanoma cell line), and 4T1 (a murine breast cancer cell line) (Fig. 3b). Thus, we believe that these fluorescent probes are robust tools for cell membrane imaging in vitro.
## 2.3. Three-dimensional (3D) visualization of the epidermal cell membranes of zebrafish embryos
The excellent in vitro imaging performance of the dendrimer-based probes encouraged us to further check whether these probes are capable of visualizing the plasma membranes of cells in vivo. Zebrafish, a commonly used transparent animal model, was chosen for this purpose. We began by bathing the zebrafish embryos at 24 hpf (hours post fertilization) in the DPC-Cy2, DPC-Cy3, or DPC-Cy5-containing medium \((60\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL})\) for \(15\;\mathrm{min}\), and the confocal images taken without washing revealed that only the chorion (the membrane surrounding the embryo) was stained (Fig. 4).
Then the zebrafish embryos at 48 hpf were also stained by DPC-Cy5 \((60\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL})\) for \(15\,\mathrm{min}\), and confocal \(\mathcal{Z}\)-stack fluorescence images at different focal planes showed that the probes were retained on the skin of the embryo without further entering the interior of the zebrafish (Fig. S7), which was mainly caused by the strong affinity between DPC-Cy5 and the plasma membrane of the epidermis. In an attempt to observe the zebrafish more clearly, 3D image reconstruction was adopted to visualize the DPC-Cy5-stained zebrafish. As displayed in
Fig. 5, the DPC-Cy5 probe clearly stained the boundary of every single cell residing on the surface of the embryo and fully visualized the zebrafish embryonic epidermis. Furthermore, we confirmed that the commercial dyes (CellMask Green and DiD) could hardly stain the zebrafish (Fig. S8). Subsequently, the DPC-Cy2 and DPC-Cy3 probes were also applied to stain the epidermis of zebrafish. The results revealed that DPC-Cy3 possessed similar staining quality to DPC-Cy5, but the DPC-Cy2-stained zebrafish resulted in an undesired image (Fig. S9a and b). Since the wavelength of the excitation laser used for DPC-Cy2 is \(488\ \mathrm{nm}\) which may also result in the autofluorescence of zebrafish, the obtained image presented high background noise. Next, we explored the long-term imaging performance of DPC-Cy5, and the corresponding results (Fig. S9c and d) suggested that the probe could stably visualize zebrafish for at least \(2\,\mathrm{~h~}\). We believe that these zebrafish probes that can realize high-quality model animal (e.g., zebrafish) imaging may find interesting applications such as nanotoxicity evaluation as illustrated below.
## 2.4. Epidermal toxicity of MOFs toward zebrafish embryos
MOFs are prepared by linking inorganic and organic units via strong bonds. This new class of porous materials is attracting intense attention due to their large pore sizes, high apparent surface areas, selective incorporation of small molecules, and optical or magnetic responses to the inclusion of guests (Furukawa et al., 2013; Rowsell and Yaghi, 2004; Stock and Biswas, 2012; Zhou et al., 2012). Generally, MOFs have been utilized in many fields such as gas storage (Rosi et al., 2003), separation (Li et al., 2009), catalysis (Long and Yaghi, 2009; Ma et al., 2009), and biomedicine (Della Rocca et al., 2011; Keskin and Kuzlel, 2011; Yang and Yang, 2020). As for the application of MOFs in biomedicine which has attracted great attention recently, the biocompatibility of MOFs should be carefully investigated. However, most researchers only focused on the cytotoxicity of MOFs in vitro, in spite of the potential
damage of MOFs in vivo (Baati et al., 2013; Ren et al., 2014; Tamames-Tabar et al., 2014). To study the potential toxicity of MOFs in vivo, Ruyra et al. incubated zebrafish embryos with various MOFs including zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) and Material of Institute Lavoisier-100 (MIL-100) NPs, and the results revealed a strong correlation between the in vitro and in vivo toxicity of the materials by comparing the hatching/survival rates of MOF-treated zebrafish with the in vitro cytotoxicity results of the MOFs (Ruyra et al., 2015). Although the hatching rate and survival rate of zebrafish can partially reflect the biocompatibility of MOFs, the doubt whether MOFs are non-toxic when the survival rate of zebrafish reaches \(100\%\) still remains. Thus, a more accurate indicator that can truly tell the health of zebrafish is required, and the zebrafish epidermis, which is the first barrier of zebrafish to aquatic infections and an important part of the fish mucosal immune system, is a satisfactory object of biocompatibility study (Jia et al., 2019b; Lazado and Caipang, 2014). Herein, ZIF-8 and MIL-100 NPs were chosen as the typical MOFs which have been widely utilized for anticancer and antibacterial applications (Cai et al., 2020; Kaur et al., 2020). The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image of MIL-100 NPs and the TEM image of ZIF-8 NPs confirmed the successful synthesis of the two MOFs (Fig. S10). According to the previous research (Ruyra et al., 2015), the zebrafish embryos in the gastrula period (7 hpf) were cultured in embryo media containing various concentrations of ZIF-8 (Zn: 10, 20, and \(50~\upmu\mathrm{M}\)) or MIL-100 (Fe: 10, 20, and \(50~\upmu\mathrm{M}\)) NPs. After \(65\ \mathrm{h}\) of culture, the hatched zebrafish embryos were stained by DPC-Cy5 NPs (60 \(\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\)) for \(15\ \mathrm{min}\) to visualize the epidermis. Interestingly, the 3D confocal images in Fig. 6 revealed that the zebrafish embryos in the ZIF-8 NP-treated groups presented many red dots (marked by white arrows) on the zebrafish surface compared with the
control group (which only had red circles on the surface of the zebrafish embryo). Considering that these red dots may indicate apoptotic epidermal cells caused by ZIF-8 NPs, we utilized the DPC-Cy5 nanoprobe to stain the apoptotic cells (using HPAEpiCs as a model) which were induced by the commonly used anticancer chemodrug doxorubicin in vitro, and the confocal imaging results revealed that both the plasma membrane and the interior of cells were stained (Fig. S11) so that the apoptotic epidermal cells seemed like red dots rather than red circles, which confirmed our above deduction. More interestingly, the morphology of some epidermal cells became severely deformed when the concentration of ZIF-8 NPs reached \(50~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) (Fig. 6d), confirming the high toxicity of ZIF-8 NPs to the epidermal cells of zebrafish. By contrast, the MIL-100 NP-treated groups (Fig. 6e–g) showed no significant difference compared with the untreated zebrafish (Fig. 6a), suggesting the good biocompatibility of the NPs at these tested concentrations (\(<50\) \(\upmu\mathrm{M}\)).
To further explore the intrinsic relationship between the visual changes of epidermal cells and the toxicity of MOFs, the traditional methods of toxicity evaluation, including the hatching rate and survival rate of zebrafish embryos, were employed for comparison purposes. Moreover, the 3D images of the zebrafish embryos treated by a higher concentration of ZIF-8 (100 \(\upmu\mathrm{M}\), based on Zn) and MIL-100 (100 \(\upmu\mathrm{M}\), based on Fe) were also recorded. The hatching rate of embryos (at 72 hpf) which is generally considered as the end of the embryonic stage was calculated, and the results suggested that ZIF-8 NP-treated groups presented a decreasing trend of the hatching rate with increasing concentrations at \(\geq20~\upmu\mathrm{M}\), while the hatching rates in all the MIL-100 NP-treated groups were kept at \(100\%\) (Fig. 7a). Besides, ZIF-8 NP-treated zebrafish embryos (at 120 hpf) revealed lower survival rates than the
corresponding hatching rates at the high concentrations of 50 and 100 \(\upmu\mathrm{M}\) (Fig. 7a and b). For example, the hatching rate of \(100\ \upmu\mathrm{M}\) ZIF-8 NP-treated zebrafish was \(50\%\) at 72 hpf, but the corresponding survival rate at 120 hpf decreased to \(30\%\) ; in contrast, the survival rate of MIL-100 NP-treated groups was still \(100\%\) at the concentration of \(100~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) (Fig. 7b). The above results obtained from traditional toxicity evaluation methods only manifested that the ZIF-8 NPs showed high toxicity at the concentrations of 50 and \(100~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) , but they could not explain the reason
of decreased survival rates compared with the corresponding hatching rates or accurately uncover whether the MOFs were toxic or not when the two rates were \(100\%\) . However, according to the confocal images of zebrafish stained by our probe (Figs. 6d and 7c), the clues for the above two issues were easy to be obtained. As displayed in Fig. 7c, at a concentration of \(100\,\upmu\mathrm{M}\) , the ZIF-8 NPs caused severe damage (as illustrated by the numerous red dots or irregular aggregates marked by yellow arrows) to the epidermal cells which are an important part of the
mucosal immune system of the zebrafish embryos to protect themselves from the environment. Thus, these zebrafish embryos without intact epidermal cells probably suffered more serious damage after hatching compared with control groups, and the damage might result in the death of zebrafish embryos which could clearly explain the lower survival rate (120 hpf) relative to the hatching rate (72 hpf). For the MIL-100 NP (100 \(\upmu\)M)-treated group, only a small quantity of red dots (apoptotic cells) could be observed on the zebrafish surface (Fig. 7c), and hence we considered that the NPs were only slightly toxic even though the corresponding survival rate (120 hpf) was 100% (Fig. 7b). Based on the above observations, we believe that the our nanoprobe could visualize the toxicity of MOFs toward zebrafish embryos by staining their epidermal cells, and this epidermal staining strategy was more precise for nanotoxicity evaluation compared with the hatching/survival rate analysis. To ensure the safe biomedical applications of MOF materials, their biocompatibility should be carefully evaluated from more aspects like the morphological changes of the epidermal cell membranes.
## 3. Conclusions
In summary, we prepared dendrimer-based multicolor fluorescent probes for staining plasma membranes of mammalian cells. These probes presented good biocompatibility due to the conjugation of PEG chains onto the dendrimers and decreased positive charges of the dendrimers after the conjugation of PEG-Chol and cyanine molecules. In our design, the following two aspects were crucial for the staining performance. On the one hand, electrostatic attraction between the positively charged probes and the negatively charged cell surfaces enabled the probes to attach to the plasma membrane. On the other hand, due to the multiple cholesterol moieties on the surface of dendrimers, the probes may have multisite membrane anchoring capacity that contributes to the desirable staining quality and long-term membrane retention. We demonstrated that the DPC-Cy5 probe realized better plasma membrane imaging performance than the commercial probes (i.e., CellMask Green and DiD). Furthermore, green (DPC-Cy2) and yellow (DPC-Cy3) fluorescent probes were also synthesized, and all these three probes achieved excellent plasma membrane imaging performance of diverse mammalian cells (HPAEpiC, B16, 4T1, and DC2.4 cells). In addition, we further verified that the DPC-Cy5 probe showed excellent plasma membrane staining of the epidermal cells in zebrafish, and the 3D confocal images of the zebrafish were proved to be able to reflect toxicity of the two MOF materials ZIF-8 NPs and MIL-100 NPs. Our strategy offered a potential way to evaluate the state of epidermal cells in zebrafish which is extremely important in fish mucosal immune system, and the results suggested that ZIF-8 NPs possessed toxicity even at low concentrations which could not be revealed by the hatching rate and survival rate analyses. Therefore, this staining strategy can more accurately judge whether the materials are toxic at a cellular level compared with traditional evaluation strategies (hatching rate and survival rate analyses), which will have great application potential in toxicity evaluation. Overall, we believe that these universal, wash-free, stable, and multicolor cell surface imaging probes will find many important biomedical applications in the future.
